<blockquote>
<p>I very much enjoy the fact that you wrote a story about a Foundation researcher carving kill-symbols into their skin without the researcher actually &quot;going crazy&quot; in the stereotypical horror sense.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Heh, I suppose that <em>is</em> kind of a subversion.</p>
<p>That's not to say that Krupin is totally unaffected by his work with memes. I tend to write experienced memeticists as highly emotional and with poor judgment because, in my headcanon, workplace safety is a lot harder when you don't always have the clearest idea of what you're working with &#8212; poring over borderline-anomalous memes, over time, sort of fries the processes of the brain involved with logically processing things, so they make logical leaps and come up with weird plans based on their intuition.</p>
<p>But you're right in that it's definitely a different brand of not being in the best state of mind.</p>

