														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2354541">
									

<p>Part of what's at issue is that the most successful character-driven things have a <em>sympathetic</em> protagonist or supporting characters. Not saying the protagonist has to be a <a href="http://tvtropes.org/pmwiki/pmwiki.php/Main/JerkWithAHeartOfGold">jerk with a heart of gold</a>, but they need to be more than excessive &quot;I'm mean!&quot;</p>
<p>If the reader can't find <em>any</em> redeeming qualities of an asshole-ish character, <em>they cannot connect with the character</em> outside of wanting to metaphorically tear their spine out and beat them to death with it. If this is the protagonist, then the work has to make it up elsewhere, maybe by making the other characters a lot worse, or by introducing a sympathetic antagonist.</p>
<p>Here, it looks like the attempt was made for the reader to sympathize with Dr. Barley, but he comes off as too unfeeling. So, our choice ends up connecting with someone who has no apparant redeming qualities, or someone with the emotional depth of tinfoil. Giving Barley too much depth would come accross as him being too unprofessional, at least for interviews, so the only choice is to try and inject sonething humanizing into Krupin.</p>
<p>And I'm just not seeing that anywhere in the article.</p>

							</div>

								
					
