														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2354297">
									

<p>Those promised additional thoughts:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I ended up liking this. I like the characterization, but I also like how the conversation was somewhat balanced between the two doctors. I mean, it was still a giant exposition dump, but it included enough back-and-forth with Dr. Barely to keep it from being excessively one-sided. You also make it fairly clear (imho) that 1: Konstantin isn't as clever as he thinks he is and 2: Barley thinks he's a prick.</p>
<p>As to the most unbelievable parts, this is very hard-meme, if we were to posit a scale ranging from soft- to hard-meme in the style of low- versus high-magic fantasy. That's not necessarily a bad thing, but it is the only card that gets played. And it gets played a lot. Oddly enough, the thing I found least believable wasn't the vocal-cord modification (which I mostly thought was gross), it was the idea of a kill-meme transmittable through a basic chat interface. I mean, ok, in my headcanon of memes you could probably ascii one up with like thirty perfectly set lines lines, but that's about it. (<a href="/scp-2999">SCP-2999</a> did anomalous things with plain text, but explicitly had non-standard characters.)</p>
<p>It's left ambiguous whether or not the text is deadly. I prefer to believe that he's full of shit, but if I have a complaint it's that it's somewhat far towards it being real for me. Can't suggest an improvement, just my reaction.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<br />
Anyhow, +1
